(1247114, 158839, 34396, '<td valign="top">I think this comes pretty close.  I see more "longing" than "bored".  A bit hard to see the dog''s facial expression because of the glare on the glass.  If the lighting was better I''d have scored it higher.</td>', 'I think this comes pretty close.  I see more "longing" than "bored".  A bit hard to see the dog''s facial expression because of the glare on the glass.  If the lighting was better I''d have scored it higher.', '2005-03-23 23:11:12.000000', NULL, 1),
(1247123, 158058, 34396, '<td valign="top">He looks more "concerned" over what is causing his grass to droop than so "bored" that he is watching it grow.  Lighting is maybe just a tiny bit bright on the background.</td>', 'He looks more "concerned" over what is causing his grass to droop than so "bored" that he is watching it grow.  Lighting is maybe just a tiny bit bright on the background.', '2005-03-23 23:15:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(1247124, 158905, 10261, '<td valign="top">I can''t figure out how this is supposed to portray boredom. Is the whiteboard bored? The pens? That paperclip, perhaps? The items depicted are rather common / boring and don''t offer any real interest.<br/> <br/>On the technical / execution side:  The picture, the camera, or both were slightly off-horizontal when this was taken, so the writing surface appears to be higher off the ground on the right side than the left. Normally I wouldn''t consider this a flaw, but when the shot is as minimalistic as this, every little detail counts. The focus is a little bit soft and the brightness &amp;/or contrast could use a boost. I''m guessing that you trusted the camera''s meter to get the correct exposure for this scene. When shooting something against a solid color like this, consider the meter a rough guess at best. One of the assumptions that all digital cameras (and film development machines, for that matter) make is that if all of the colors and brightness levels in the scene are averaged, the result will be a solid grey image (this is called the "grey world assumption"). In practice what this means is that if you set your camera to fully automatic mode and take two pictures, one of a solid black wall and one of a solid white wall, both of pictures will appear to be of the same solid grey wall. Because your picture consisted largely of a solid white surface, your camera adjusted the brightness level down toward grey.</td>', 'I can''t figure out how this is supposed to portray boredom. Is the whiteboard bored? The pens? That paperclip, perhaps? The items depicted are rather common / boring and don''t offer any real interest. On the technical / execution side:  The picture, the camera, or both were slightly off-horizontal when this was taken, so the writing surface appears to be higher off the ground on the right side than the left. Normally I wouldn''t consider this a flaw, but when the shot is as minimalistic as this, every little detail counts. The focus is a little bit soft and the brightness &/or contrast could use a boost. I''m guessing that you trusted the camera''s meter to get the correct exposure for this scene. When shooting something against a solid color like this, consider the meter a rough guess at best. One of the assumptions that all digital cameras (and film development machines, for that matter) make is that if all of the colors and brightness levels in the scene are averaged, the result will be a solid grey image (this is called the "grey world assumption"). In practice what this means is that if you set your camera to fully automatic mode and take two pictures, one of a solid black wall and one of a solid white wall, both of pictures will appear to be of the same solid grey wall. Because your picture consisted largely of a solid white surface, your camera adjusted the brightness level down toward grey.', '2005-03-23 23:20:13.000000', NULL, 1),
